      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of the base. The base is a square with side length 10 cm.\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the base} = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of one of the triangular faces. Each triangular face is an isosceles triangle with a base of 10 cm and a height (slant height) that we need to determine.\n\nTo find the slant height, we use the Pythagorean theorem. The slant height is the altitude of a right triangle formed by the peak of the pyramid, the center of the base, and the midpoint of one of the base edges. The distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of one of the base sides is half the side length of the base, which is:\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\nThe height of this right triangle is the slant height of the pyramid, which is 12 cm. Let's call the slant height \\( l \\). According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nl^2 = 12^2 + 5^2 = 144 + 25 = 169\n\\]\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\nSo, the area of one triangular face is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\nSince there are four triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of the four triangular faces} = 4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the total area of the four triangular faces:\n\\[\n\\text{Total surface area} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{360}\n\\]",
