      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the lateral surface area separately, and then sum them up.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the base:**\n   The base is a square with side length 10 cm.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the base} = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one of the triangular faces:**\n   The height of the triangular face from the base to the apex is 12 cm. The distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of one of the base edges (which is the slant height of the triangular face) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by half the side of the base, the height of the pyramid, and the slant height.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Half the side of the base} = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Slant height} = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   The area of one triangular face is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n   Since there are four triangular faces, the total area of the lateral surface is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Lateral surface area} = 4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total surface area:**\n   The total surface area is the sum of the area of the base and the lateral surface area.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total surface area} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\) square centimeters.",
